First, the requirements. The parser must read from any file-like object, emit records as lists of strings, handle double-quote escaping ("" inside a quoted field is a literal "), support arbitrary delimiters, and never hold more than one record plus a fixed-size read buffer in memory. It should also be strict about malformed input by default, with an option to be lenient.

The state machine. The cleanest way to implement this is a character-level state machine with four states: FIELD_START, IN_FIELD, IN_QUOTED, and QUOTE_IN_QUOTED. FIELD_START is the state at the beginning of a field. IN_FIELD means we are reading an unquoted field. IN_QUOTED means we are inside a quoted section. QUOTE_IN_QUOTED means we just saw a quote inside a quoted section; the next character decides whether it was an escaped quote (another quote follows), the end of the field (delimiter or newline), or an error (anything else, under strict mode).

Here is the implementation. Note that we read the input in fixed-size chunks and iterate character by character; this keeps memory bounded even for a 100 GB file, because the chunk buffer is discarded after processing.

