Introduction

A lock-free ring buffer is a bounded circular queue where producers and consumers make progress without blocking each other. The key insight is that the buffer's fixed size lets us use simple counters as indices, and single-producer/single-consumer (SPSC) configurations can be implemented with only atomic loads and stores—no compare-and-swap loops, no locks. The hard part is not the data structure; it's the memory ordering: ensuring that when a producer publishes an item, the consumer's subsequent read of that item is guaranteed to see it, across cores and compilers.

